+# CSV contact import
+
+## Why
+
+Sales keeps leads in spreadsheets. Today they add those contacts to contactbook one
+at a time. They need to load a whole CSV file at once and see exactly what happened:
+which contacts were created, which were updated, which rows were rejected and why.
+That way they can fix the bad rows in the spreadsheet and import again.
+
+The file uses the same columns as our CSV export (`name,email,phone,tags`). It can
+also be a file from the Outlook / Google Contacts address-book export script, which
+has `first_name` and `last_name` instead of `name`. Most files will come from Excel.
+
+## Interface
+
+Other code calls exactly this:
+
+- `src/contactbook/importer.py`: `import_contacts(repo: InMemoryContactRepository, data: str | bytes) -> ImportReport`
+  where `data` is the file content: raw bytes as read from disk, or already-decoded text.
+- `ImportReport` (dataclass, same module): `created: list[str]`, `updated: list[str]` (normalised
+  emails), `errors: list[RowIssue]`, `warnings: list[RowIssue]`.
+- `RowIssue` (dataclass, same module): `row: int`, `column: str | None`, `message: str`.
+- `ImportFileError(ContactError)` in `src/contactbook/errors.py`, raised when the file as a whole
+  can't be imported.
+
+## Terms
+
+- **Row number**: the 1-based number of the physical line in the file. Every line
+  counts, including blank lines. The header is row 1 and the first line after it is
+  row 2. If a quoted cell contains line breaks, the record gets the number of the
+  line it starts on, and later records keep counting physical lines. So row numbers
+  match a text editor's line numbers.
+- **Blank row**: an empty line, or a record where every cell is empty or contains
+  only whitespace.
+- **Contact row**: any record after the header that is not blank, whether or not it
+  turns out to be valid.
+- **Column name** in a `RowIssue`: one of `name`, `email`, `phone`, `tags`, or `None`
+  when the problem is not tied to one column.
+- **Applied row**: a row that created or updated a contact. That includes rows that
+  only got a phone warning.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+### Input and encoding
+
+1. `data` can be `bytes` or `str`. The same content gives the same result either way.
+2. `bytes` are read as UTF-8. A leading UTF-8 BOM is ignored.
+3. A `str` that starts with the BOM character (`﻿`) is handled as if the BOM
+   were absent.
+4. If `bytes` are not valid UTF-8, the import raises `ImportFileError`. Its message
+   tells the user to re-save the file as "CSV UTF-8".
+5. The delimiter is a comma. Fields can be quoted the standard CSV way: a quoted
+   field can contain commas, `;`, line breaks, and doubled `""` quotes.
+6. Both `\n` and `\r\n` line endings work.
+
+### Header
+
+7. The first line is the header and is required. If the file is completely empty,
+   or has no non-blank line, the import raises `ImportFileError`.
+8. Header names are matched case-insensitively and ignoring surrounding whitespace,
+   so `" Email "` is the `email` column. Columns can be in any order.
+9. `email` is required. If it is missing, the import raises `ImportFileError`.
+10. A name is required. It comes from a `name` column, or from the pair `first_name`
+    and `last_name`. If there is no `name` column and the pair is incomplete or
+    absent, the import raises `ImportFileError`.
+11. If `name` and `first_name`/`last_name` are all present, `name` is used and the
+    other two are ignored.
+12. `phone` and `tags` are optional columns.
+13. Unknown extra columns are ignored. They never cause an error. Ignoring them
+    silently or with a warning are both acceptable.
+14. If the same column appears twice in the header (after case and space matching),
+    the import raises `ImportFileError`.
+15. A header-only file (or a header followed only by blank rows) returns an empty
+    report: no created, updated, errors or warnings. The repository is unchanged.
+
+### Whole-file rejection
+
+16. A file with more than 1000 contact rows raises `ImportFileError`. Blank rows do
+    not count. Rows that would turn out to be errors do count. A file with exactly
+    1000 contact rows is accepted.
+17. If a quoted field is never closed, the import raises `ImportFileError`.
+18. Whenever `ImportFileError` is raised, the repository is exactly as it was before
+    the call. No row is applied.
+
+### Rows: general
+
+19. Blank rows are skipped silently. They produce no entry in any report list, but
+    they still count for row numbering.
+20. Each row is handled independently. A row with an error is not applied at all.
+    That includes the case where its email matches an existing contact: none of its
+    fields change that contact. Other rows are still applied.
+21. Every rejected row produces at least one `RowIssue` in `errors` with that row's
+    number. A row with several problems can produce one entry or several.
+22. A row with more cells than the header is an error with column `None`.
+23. A row with fewer cells than the header is treated as if the missing trailing
+    cells were empty.
+24. `created` and `updated` contain normalised emails (trimmed, lower-cased), in file
+    order. An email appears at most once across both lists.
+
+### Rows: email
+
+25. The email is normalised with the existing email validator. That means
+    surrounding spaces and capitalisation make no difference.
+26. A missing, empty or invalid email is an error on column `email`.
+27. The first applied row for an email wins. Every later row with the same
+    normalised email is an error on column `email` and is not applied. This holds
+    whether the first row created the contact or updated an existing one, and also
+    if the first row got a phone warning.
+28. If an earlier row with that email was rejected, a later valid row with the same
+    email is applied normally.
+
+### Rows: name
+
+29. The name is required on every row, including rows that update an existing
+    contact. It is validated with the existing name validator: outer spaces are
+    trimmed and inner runs are collapsed. If it is empty or invalid, the row is an
+    error on column `name`.
+30. With `first_name`/`last_name`, the name is the first and last name joined with
+    one space. If one of the two is empty, the name is the other one. If both are
+    empty, the row is an error on column `name`. Any name problem in such a file is
+    reported on column `name`.
+31. For an existing contact, the file's name replaces the stored name.
+
+### Rows: phone
+
+32. A non-empty phone cell is validated with the existing phone validator. For an
+    existing contact, a valid phone replaces the stored phone. A new contact gets
+    that phone.
+33. An empty phone cell, or a missing `phone` column, leaves the stored phone
+    unchanged. A new contact has no phone.
+34. An invalid phone does not reject the row. The rest of the row is still applied
+    (created or updated), and the phone is left as it was (none for a new contact).
+    The row gets a `RowIssue` in `warnings` with column `phone`. The row's email is
+    in `created` or `updated` as usual.
+35. A phone failure is the only cause of warnings, apart from the optional
+    unknown-column notice in criterion 13.
+
+### Rows: tags
+
+36. The tags cell is split on `;`. Each piece is normalised with the existing tag
+    validator. Empty pieces and repeats are ignored.
+37. If any tag is invalid, the whole row is an error on column `tags` and is not
+    applied.
+38. For an existing contact, the file's tags are added to the stored tags. An import
+    never removes tags. An empty tags cell, or a missing `tags` column, leaves the
+    stored tags unchanged.
+
+### Created vs. updated
+
+39. A valid row whose email is not yet in the repository creates a contact, and its
+    email is listed in `created`.
+40. A valid row whose email already exists (case-insensitive) updates that contact.
+    It never creates a second contact and is never an error for that reason. If the
+    row changes the contact, its email is listed in `updated`. A row that changes
+    nothing can either be listed in `updated` or left out of both lists. Both are
+    acceptable.
+41. All stored values are normalised the same way as contacts added through
+    `ContactBook.add`.
+
+### Round trip
+
+42. Importing the output of `export_csv` into an empty repository reproduces the
+    same contacts (name, email, phone, tags).
+43. Importing the output of `export_csv` back into the repository it came from
+    leaves the repository unchanged: the same contacts with the same values, and no
+    contacts added. `created`, `errors` and `warnings` are empty. This criterion says
+    nothing about `updated` (see criterion 40).
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- No CLI command, HTTP endpoint or `ContactBook` method for import. Only the
+  function above.
+- No deleting of contacts that are missing from the file.
+- No dry-run or preview mode.
+- An import never clears a phone or removes tags.
+- No fields beyond name, email, phone and tags.
+- No delimiters other than comma, and no encodings other than UTF-8. No guessing of
+  encoding or delimiter.
+- No files over 1000 contact rows. Users split them.
+- No thread safety beyond what `InMemoryContactRepository` already offers.
